---
import PrimerChat from "../PrimerChat.astro";
import { aiCardId, archiveAi } from "../../../lib/archive-ai";

const card = archiveAi.idea;

const readings = [10, 14];
const avg = readings.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / readings.length;
const f1 = (n: number) => n.toFixed(1);
const runs = [
  { out: `${f1((avg * 9) / 5)} °F`, code: "v1, calculated 12 × 9/5", diff: true },
  { out: `${f1((avg * 9) / 5 + 32)} °F`, code: "v2, calculated 12 × 9/5 + 32", diff: true },
];

const scheduled = 8 * 60;
const arrivals = ["08:02", "08:05"];
const mins = (t: string) => Number(t.slice(0, 2)) * 60 + Number(t.slice(3));
const delays = arrivals.map((t) => mins(t) - scheduled);
const thresholds = [2, 5];
const late = (th: number) => delays.filter((d) => d >= th).length;
const plural = (n: number) => `${n} late arrival${n === 1 ? "" : "s"}`;

const original = [4, 6];
const revised = [4, 9];
const sum = (xs: number[]) => xs.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
const states = [
  { id: "kept", label: "Original batch saved", batch: original, gone: null },
  { id: "over", label: "Overwritten by the revised batch", batch: revised, gone: original[1] },
];

const checks = [
  ["Reproduce 21.6 °F", `In the weather example, what would you need to keep to reproduce the original ${runs[0].out} output after deleting it?`],
  ["Two counts", "In the bus example, which part of the output record explains why the two counts differ?"],
  ["After the overwrite", "In the water-meter example, after the overwrite, what total can the fixed code calculate from the saved batch, and why can’t it recover the original total?"],
];
---

<PrimerChat
  prompt={card.prompt}
  module="Module 2"
  back={`../02-archive/#${aiCardId("idea")}`}
  about="This is a reply an AI assistant gave to Module 2's raw-and-derived study prompt, lightly edited. Send the same prompt yourself and you will get a different reply."
  turn="In a live chat, you would answer those three questions and the assistant would review your reasoning. To try that, copy the prompt into your own AI assistant. Then go back to Part 2 and add a fourth situation to the research team's check: the cleaning script was lost. What can the team still answer?"
>
  <h2 class="pr-title">Raw data, derived data and replay</h2>
  <p><strong>Raw data</strong> is what your system receives from a source. Save an exact copy at intake, before your processing code parses, cleans, rounds, or filters it. If that code has a bug, the saved copy gives you the original input to try again.</p>
  <p><strong>Derived data</strong> is what your code produces from those inputs, such as a total, a conversion, or a count. You can delete and rebuild it if you keep the raw inputs, the code version, and the settings that produced it.</p>
  <p class="pr-pull">Each output should record those three things so you can reproduce it and explain why it might differ from another output.</p>

  <h2>1. Weather station: fixing a conversion bug</h2>
  <p>A station sends two readings, <code>{f1(readings[0])} °C</code> and <code>{f1(readings[1])} °C</code>. You save the two messages exactly as received under <code>weather-01</code>.</p>
  <p>The average is <code>({readings.join(" + ")}) ÷ {readings.length} = {avg} °C</code>. Your code converts the average to Fahrenheit, but version 1 forgets to add 32. You fix the formula in version 2 and replay the saved messages.</p>
  <figure class="pr-card">
    <p class="pr-label">Two outputs and their records</p>
    <div class="pa-prov">
      {runs.map((r) => (
        <div>
          <p class="pa-prov-head"><b>Derived output</b><strong>{r.out}</strong></p>
          <dl>
            <div><dt>Raw input</dt><dd>weather-01</dd></div>
            <div class="is-diff"><dt>Code</dt><dd>{r.code}</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Settings</dt><dd>Fahrenheit, one decimal</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <figcaption>Same input, same settings. Only the code line differs between the two records.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>The records show that both runs used the same input and settings. The formula change explains the disagreement. If you deleted either output, you could reproduce it by running its recorded code version with those inputs and settings.</p>

  <h2>2. Public transit: changing a setting</h2>
  <p>A bus feed sends two arrival times, <code>{arrivals[0]}</code> and <code>{arrivals[1]}</code>, for buses both scheduled at <code>08:00</code>. You save the feed as <code>arrivals-01</code>. The code calculates delays of {delays[0]} and {delays[1]} minutes, then counts how many meet the configured threshold.</p>
  <figure class="pr-card pa-bus">
    <fieldset class="pr-seg">
      <legend>Setting: late means at least</legend>
      {thresholds.map((th, i) => <label><input type="radio" name="pa-raw-th" value={`t${th}`} checked={i === 0} /><span>{th} minutes</span></label>)}
    </fieldset>
    {thresholds.map((th) => (
      <div class="pa-bus-view" data-th={`t${th}`}>
        <ul class="pa-rows">
          {arrivals.map((t, i) => <li><span>Bus {i + 1} · arrived {t} · {delays[i]} min</span><span class:list={["pr-chip", delays[i] >= th ? "is-bad" : "is-ok"]}>{delays[i] >= th ? "Late" : "Not late"}</span></li>)}
        </ul>
        <p class="pa-total"><span>Derived output from <code>arrivals-01</code>, code v1</span><b>{plural(late(th))}</b></p>
      </div>
    ))}
    <figcaption>Same input, same code. The setting is the only line of the record that differs.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>Both counts follow their recorded settings. If the first report was supposed to use the {thresholds[1]}-minute threshold, replaying <code>arrivals-01</code> with that setting corrects the report. If the feed briefly showed <code>08:03</code> for the first bus and you never saved that version, replay has no copy of that earlier reading to work from.</p>

  <h2>3. Water monitoring: losing the original input</h2>
  <p>A water meter sends an original batch of <code>{original[0]} L</code> and <code>{original[1]} L</code>. You save it, and version 1 publishes a total of <code>{original[0]} L</code> because a bug skips the second reading. With the original batch available, version 2 could replay it and calculate <code>{original.join(" + ")} = {sum(original)} L</code>.</p>
  <p>Now suppose a revised batch arrives with <code>{revised[0]} L</code> and <code>{revised[1]} L</code>, and you overwrite your only copy of the original. Version 2 can calculate <code>{revised.join(" + ")} = {sum(revised)} L</code> from the revised batch. The old <code>{original[0]} L</code> output gives you no way to recover the skipped <code>{original[1]} L</code>. Recording a distinct input version for each output would show which batch each run used. Keeping both versions would let you replay either one.</p>
  <figure class="pr-card pa-water">
    <fieldset class="pr-seg">
      <legend>What the archive holds</legend>
      {states.map((st, i) => <label><input type="radio" name="pa-raw-water" value={st.id} checked={i === 0} /><span>{st.label}</span></label>)}
    </fieldset>
    {states.map((st) => (
      <div class="pa-water-view" data-water={st.id}>
        <ul class="pa-rows">
          <li><span>Published by v1</span><b>{original[0]} L</b></li>
          <li><span>Saved batch</span><b>{st.batch.map((x) => `${x} L`).join(" · ")}</b></li>
          <li class="is-fix"><span>v2 replays the saved batch</span><b>{st.batch.join(" + ")} = {sum(st.batch)} L</b></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="pa-water-say">{st.gone === null
          ? `The replay corrects the very total v1 published: ${sum(original)} L.`
          : `The replay gives the revised batch's total. The skipped ${st.gone} L reading of the original is gone, so v1's total can't be corrected.`}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </figure>
  <aside class="pr-course" aria-label="Course note">
    <p class="pr-label">Course note</p>
    <p>In Tuesday's story, the raw data is the schedule pages Kick saved on Friday. Monday's table and Tuesday's table are both derived: each is a calculation over those pages, the parser code and its config. The tidy rule fix is the weather station's: one code change, replayed over the same saved input.</p>
    <p>Dunmore's page is the reply's unsaved <code>08:03</code> reading. It was never saved on Friday, so no replay recovers it. Its results still come from the visitors' saved pages, as they did on Monday.</p>
    <p>For the research team's check, sort its four kept things the reply's way: which is the raw input, which is code, and which are derived. Then ask which of them a reviewer needs to see how a chart number was made.</p>
  </aside>

  <h2>4. Check your understanding</h2>
  <ol class="pr-checks">{checks.map(([name, q]) => <li><b>{name}</b><span>{q}</span></li>)}</ol>
</PrimerChat>

<style>
  .pa-prov { display: grid; gap: 0.6rem; }
  @media (min-width: 46rem) { .pa-prov { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }
  .pa-prov > div { padding: 0.6rem 0.8rem; border: 1px solid var(--tl-line); border-radius: 8px; background: var(--tl-bg); }
  :global(.pr) .pa-prov-head { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: baseline; margin: 0 0 0.4rem; }
  .pa-prov-head b { color: var(--tl-muted); font-size: 0.85rem; }
  .pa-prov-head strong { color: var(--sl-color-white); font-size: 1.4rem; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .pa-prov dl { display: grid; gap: 0.25rem; margin: 0; font-size: 0.88rem; }
  .pa-prov dl div { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 6rem 1fr; gap: 0.5rem; padding: 0.2rem 0.45rem; border-radius: 6px; }
  .pa-prov dt { color: var(--tl-muted); }
  .pa-prov dd { margin: 0; }
  .pa-prov .is-diff { background: color-mix(in srgb, #d9822b 18%, transparent); font-weight: 600; }
  .pa-bus .pr-seg, .pa-water .pr-seg { margin-bottom: 0.7rem; }
  .pa-bus-view, .pa-water-view { display: none; }
  .pa-bus:has([value="t2"]:checked) [data-th="t2"],
  .pa-bus:has([value="t5"]:checked) [data-th="t5"],
  .pa-water:has([value="kept"]:checked) [data-water="kept"],
  .pa-water:has([value="over"]:checked) [data-water="over"] { display: block; }
  .pa-rows { display: grid; gap: 0.35rem; margin: 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .pa-rows li { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; gap: 0.3rem 0.6rem; padding: 0.4rem 0.65rem; border-radius: 6px; background: var(--tl-bg); }
  .pa-rows li b { color: var(--sl-color-white); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .pa-rows li.is-fix { box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--pr-green); }
  :global(.pr) .pa-total { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: space-between; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.3rem 0.6rem; margin: 0.6rem 0 0; font-size: 0.88rem; color: var(--tl-muted); }
  .pa-total b { color: var(--sl-color-white); font-size: 1.2rem; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-water-say { margin: 0.6rem 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; }
</style>
